name: sdh-agent-collaboration
description: >
  Use when development work is split across two or more AI agents by role —
  one drafts the spec, another implements it, then the result is reviewed.
  Covers the Architect / Implementer / Reviewer roles (recommended default:
  Claude specs and optionally reviews, GPT-5.6 implements and closes), the spec-plus-a-note handoff
  contract, the three-pass review loop with a shared-contract integrity
  check, and how retro ownership works across agents. An overlay on
  sdh-release-lifecycle and sdh-phase-workflow, not a replacement.
---

Use this when a task or release is worked by **more than one AI agent**, each in a
distinct role, rather than a single agent doing everything. It maps the roles onto
the existing flow — it does **not** replace it: scoping still follows
`sdh-phase-workflow`, the release still runs through `sdh-release-lifecycle`, the
retro still follows `sdh-retro`. This skill only says **who does which part, and how
the handoff between agents works.**

Solo (one agent in all roles) is the default and needs none of this. Reach for it the
moment a second agent enters the loop.

## The three roles

Roles are abstract — assign them to whatever models you run. The **recommended
default mapping**:

| Role | Default agent | Owns |
|---|---|---|
| **Architect** | Claude | Scoping, the written spec, closing the release |
| **Implementer** | GPT-5.6 | Writing the code/content to the spec |
| **Reviewer** | by default the Architect — but either agent, or both, may review | Verifying the result against the spec + contracts |

Review is done by the spec's author by default (it holds the design intent), but this
is **not fixed**: both agents may review (cross-review), or either one. The
**Implementer is always a separate agent** from the spec's author, so the build and
its verification are never the same hands.

## The loop (flow)

1. **Architect scopes + specs** (with the user), through Analysis → Spec → Spec-review
   of `sdh-release-lifecycle` / `sdh-phase-workflow`, and gets the user's OK **on the
   spec**. Nothing goes to the Implementer before the spec is approved.
2. **Handoff.** The Architect hands over the **approved spec, optionally plus a short
   pointer note** for the next side (see the handoff contract below).
3. **Implementer builds.** Task by task, tests first, a commit per task, to the spec.
   - The **breakdown into tasks/steps is the Implementer's own working artifact** — it
     needs no user sign-off. The user's single gate was the spec.
   - The Implementer **stops for the user only at a fork the spec does not cover**. A
     question it can answer itself → it answers and continues; no "let me ask myself
     out loud and pause" stops.
   - **A spec that disagrees with the code is not a fork.** The spec says `A`, the code
     actually has `B` → that is a factual error in the spec, and it needs no
     permission: correct to what the code says, continue, and name the correction in
     the report. Stop only if the correction implies a design choice — semantics
     changed, a shared contract is touched, the behaviour became different — never for
     a changed name, path or call signature.
   - **The report lists every behavioural change made on a shared path as its own
     numbered deviation** — including changes that look mechanical (a changed
     condition, a widened guard). The Reviewer reads the report as a map only if it
     is complete; an unlisted change is a finding.
   - If the spec is ambiguous, contradicts a test, or has a gap, the Implementer does
     **not** patch the spec silently — it **raises it to the Architect**, and the
     **Architect decides who fixes the spec** (fixes it themselves, or hands it back).
4. **Architect review — optional.** The Architect may review the result (three passes,
   in order, below); it is **not a mandatory gate**. Any findings go **back to the
   Implementer** for rework, then re-review. The **shared-contract integrity** pass is
   recommended whenever the change touches a contract shared between agents, even when
   the overall review is skipped.
5. **Implementer closes.** Once any review is clean, the Implementer runs Test →
   Closure docs (stages 8–9), then hands the **next-release plan** (stage 10) back to
   the Architect. The git canon block (tag / master-merge / push) stays **user-only**.
   - **Cleanup after the tag.** The Architect removes the Implementers' worktrees and
     branches at closure, after the tag (`git worktree remove --force`,
     `git branch -D`). A squash merge means `git cherry` still lists their commits as
     `+` — expected, not unmerged work. A worktree found after a compaction is the
     footprint of the run, not an anomaly.
6. **Retro** — see ownership below.

Stage ownership on the lifecycle: the Architect owns stages 1–6 and 10 (next-release
plan), plus an optional review of the result; the Implementer owns stages 7–9 (build,
test, closure docs) and leads the release up to the git block. Retro (stage 11) follows
the closer per Retro ownership below. No role runs the tag / master-merge — that is
always the user's.

## Handoff contract — the spec, plus an optional note

The Implementer does **not** inherit the Architect's session, memory, or chat. Its
context is the **spec**, optionally with a **short handoff note** — a focused pointer
("watch X", "start with Y", context the spec doesn't carry). That's "spec + an
optional pointer," never "spec and nothing else" — but the note is a pointer, **not a
substitute** for a self-sufficient spec. The spec itself must stand alone:

- Full file paths, not "the sync module."
- Every decision with its rationale — the Implementer follows the spec **literally**,
  so an unstated assumption becomes a wrong build.
- A `## Verification` section it can run itself, written with **checking, not
  applying** commands (`cargo fmt --check`, never `cargo fmt`). An applying formatter
  masks its own diff, and review then sees a clean tree where the work did not match
  the convention.
- A case that asserts several conditions either splits into one case per condition
  or, on failure, prints the state it saw (status, tool errors, rows, values) — the
  run names the failed condition, nobody guesses it from the code.
- **Задание, указывающее на образец, называет известные дефекты образца и то,
  какие из них воспроизводить не надо.** Заведённая на такой дефект карточка
  этого не заменяет: исполнитель её не читает.
- **Grounded in the actual codebase.** Before writing the spec, the Architect greps a
  neighbour file for the real conventions (serde/naming/API shape) and writes those
  into the spec — the Implementer matches the spec, not the surrounding code it never
  reads. The same grounding applies to **any claim about what already exists** — "this
  function does X", "covered by N tests", "the field is already read" — and it is
  written concretely: full path, real symbol name, line number. The same applies to a
  claim about what surrounds the code — the runtime's lifecycle (what is loaded when,
  in which event), a limit in a contract file, that a user can actually reach the
  setting the spec says they will feel: verified at its source — the platform
  documentation, the contract, the surface code — and cited in the spec before
  handoff. The Implementer builds exactly what is written; a wish written as a fact
  becomes a faithful build of the wish. The spec is the only
  artifact that survives both a handoff to another agent **and** a compaction of its
  own author: after one, the codebase is gone from context and a grounded spec is what
  makes re-reading unnecessary, while a vague one ("the sync module") forces it.
  Список потребителей значения строится поиском и по имени константы, и по самому числу.
- No aspirational claims. "Handle all the lanes" that a test contradicts is a spec
  bug; assert only what is true and testable.
- Записка называет, кто делает коммит версии до первого выката. Если исполнитель
  закрытие не ведёт, бамп и Changelog — за архитектором до вручения акта, иначе
  гвард выката упадёт на константе.

If the context won't fit into spec + a short note, the task isn't specced enough to
hand off yet — that's the signal, not a reason to backchannel it.

## Reviewer mandate — three passes

**Scale the reviewers to the scope.** Whole-project or multi-domain review → dispatch
**3-4 reviewers in parallel, one per domain**, not a single general one. Measured on
the same code: 32 findings against 9. The three passes below stay — each reviewer runs
them inside its own domain.

**Verify a critical finding before it reaches the owner.** Open the location the
reviewer cited and confirm it exists and says what was claimed. The false-positive rate
is not zero, and a critical finding spends the owner's attention first.

Review the **result**, in this order:

1. **Spec compliance** — does it do what the spec said, no more, no less? Extra
   unrequested work is a finding too. A deviation the Implementer words as "kept the
   existing separate behaviour/geometry of X" is a finding until the Reviewer has
   asked why X is separate and got an answer from the spec or the code — the wording
   makes a divergence look deliberate (an artist composer shipped without voice on
   the phone behind exactly that sentence).
2. **Shared-contract integrity** — the highest-value pass. For every shared accessor,
   cross-repo contract, or broadcast edge the change touched: **grep ALL its consumers
   and confirm the design promise still holds.** The most-repeated escape in this
   project is a narrowing done for one consumer that silently dropped another — and it
   kept reaching the user instead of being caught in review. This pass exists for
   exactly that.
3. **Code quality** — correctness, clarity, tests that test behaviour not
   implementation.

Verify that test data satisfies the operation’s prerequisites and can reach the expected outcome. Do not check only field names and response shapes.

On a finding:
- **In-scope defect** → back to the Implementer for rework, then re-review. The
  Reviewer does **not** silently fix it — that erases attribution and hides the gap.
- **Out-of-scope** finding → a `T-NNNNNN` in `docs/todo.md`, never a silent fold.
- A finding whose fix widens or narrows a predicate names, in its fix, every reader of that same meaning (send and retry, list and card button) found by searching for the meaning, not the symbol — a fix aimed at one reader opens one door of two.

## Retro ownership

The retro follows `sdh-retro` and is **expected at closure unless the user explicitly
skips it**. In a multi-agent run it **may** split by role — each agent from its own
vantage into its own memory — but it **does not have to**: **one agent writing the
whole retro is enough, and is the default.** Who writes it (one agent or both) is the
user's call; absent a request, the agent that closes the release writes it. A solo run
keeps `sdh-retro` unchanged.

Memory is ordinary Markdown on disk, not one agent's privilege: whoever closes writes
the whole retro, project memory included, into that project's memory directory. Skill
lessons it does not edit but **nominates** — the "Editing a skill" gate in the global
rules applies identically to every role.
